version: 3
metadata:
  name: camera_smoke
  title: Does offscreen rendering work on the node this job landed on?
  description: |
    The smallest campaign that renders. One container, one camera, no system under test, no ROS.

    It exists because a camera world reached the cluster before any campaign had ever rendered a
    frame there, and died with `mujoco.FatalError: gladLoadGL error` — MuJoCo binds its GL backend
    at `import mujoco`, so every camera-less campaign before it had run happily against a backend
    that was already wrong. Nothing in RoboVAST's own examples configured a camera, so nothing
    caught it.

    Run this before trusting a rendering campaign on a lane you have not rendered on. It answers in
    a couple of minutes what a real campaign answers in an hour, and it produces a file rather than
    an opinion: `coverage/report.json` is written by a probe that renders at `configure` time, so
    its presence means a GL context was created and drawn into.

    Stepped (`mode: base`) on purpose: the simulator shares the scenario's process, so there is one
    container and one image, and a failure has almost nowhere else to come from.
  keywords: [robotics, mujoco, roqsim, camera, rendering, opengl, smoke-test]
configuration:
- name: render
execution:
  mode: base
  containers:
  # Stepped, so this collapses into the scenario's container and its image is the combined
  # robovast_roqsim one. The backend supplies the command, the GL libraries and the recording
  # variables; this file states only which simulator and which world.
    simulation:
      backend: roqsim
      config: world/camera_smoke.yaml
  # No image named anywhere in this file: the backend supplies the family member and the
  # deployment supplies the project (ROBOVAST_PROJECT), which is what lets one smoke test run
  # against whichever registry the operator publishes to.
    scenario:
      resources:
        cpu: 2
        memory: 2Gi
  scenario_file: scenario.osc
  runs: 1
  # Software rendering through llvmpipe is slow, and the first render also compiles the world.
  # Generous, but bounded: without a timeout `get_campaign_status` can return no stall verdict at
  # all, so a wedged run looks exactly like a slow one.
  timeout: 600
